# Accessibility baseline (ArchLucid UI)

## Button vs link — affordance must match behavior

**Rule:** Button = do something. Link = go somewhere. Same-page jump = link.

- **`<Link>` / `<a>`** — navigates: to another route, to an in-page anchor (`#section-id`), or opens an external URL. Style as a link (underline or link-color token), not a filled/primary button, even when it visually sits among buttons.
- **`<Button>`** — performs an in-place action: exports, generates, saves, submits, opens a modal, mutates data, or calls an API.
- **Disclosure toggle** (expands/reveals previously-hidden content, e.g. an accordion or `<details>/<summary>`) — button semantics with `aria-expanded`, because it changes page state. This is distinct from a same-page anchor jump to *already-rendered* content, which is a link.

Before styling a control, ask: does activating it move the viewport to existing content (link), reveal new content (disclosure button), or trigger a side effect (action button)? Match the element and styling to the answer — don't default to `<Button>` because it's inside a toolbar or action row.

**Reference implementation:** `archlucid-ui/src/components/RunDetailSectionNav.tsx` — in-page section jumps rendered as `<a href="#id">` with underline styling, `preventDefault()` + `scrollIntoView({ behavior: "smooth" })`, and `aria-current="page"` on the active item. Follow this pattern for any new "jump to section" control instead of a `Button`.

```tsx
// BAD — same-page jump styled and rendered as a primary action
<Button variant="default" onClick={() => scrollTo("exports")}>Open Export Section</Button>

// GOOD — same-page jump as a link
<a
  href="#exports"
  className="text-al-link underline-offset-2 hover:underline"
  onClick={(e) => {
    e.preventDefault();
    document.getElementById("exports")?.scrollIntoView({ behavior: "smooth", block: "start" });
  }}
>
  Jump to exports
</a>
```

## Other baseline rules

- Never use a clickable `<div>` or `<span>` for navigation or actions — use `<a>`/`<Link>` or `<button>` so the control is keyboard-reachable and has an implicit role.
- Every interactive control needs an accessible name (visible text, or `aria-label` when the visual label is icon-only).
- Preserve visible `:focus-visible` styling — don't suppress the default ring without providing a replacement (see `focus-visible:ring-2` on `buttonVariants` in `src/components/ui/button.tsx`).
- When a same-page jump target was previously off-screen/hidden, ensure the target receives focus (or is at least programmatically reachable) after the jump, not just visually scrolled into view.
